\subsection{A Customized Graph Contrastive Learning Framework}\label{sec:approach}
% In this section, we introduce our proposed framework. There are several unique challenges in this problem. First, there are no negative samples in the existing complex dataset.Second, maintaining the structural information of the protein-ligand complex is very important.Third, the features learned during pretraining should ideally incorporate the characteristics of the downstream task of binding affinity prediction.
% First, the number of labeled complexes with ground truth affinity scores is much smaller compared with the total number of complexes in the PDB dataset. Most deep neural network models require sufficient labels to perform well. Second, there is no natural definition of positive and negative complex pairs in the unlabeled protein-ligand complex data. Existing techniques to create positive and negative samples in graph contrast learning (GCL), such as edge perturbation and node dropping, could create an augmented complex that violates the biochemistry constraints. Third, the spatial structural interaction between a protein graph and a ligand graph and the thermal motion of atoms causes continuous, slight variations in atomic positions. The definition of negativeness in the contrastive loss may not be binary. Instead, there may be different extents of negative complexes. 

