% Exposure bias describes the situation that context words are selected from the ground truth sentence during the training phase, but from the last predicted sequence during inference. This inconsistency is known as the exposure bias problem as described by \citep{ranzato2015sequence}. Consider a scenario where a state A can transition to either state (B, B) or (C, C), resulting in two acceptable sequences: (A, B, B) and (A, C, C). Given (A) as the raw input, the model might predict `C' as the second token since it is also a reasonable prediction. However, if (A, B, B) is the reference sequence in training, (A, C) will be corrected to (A, B) for the third token prediction. Given (A, B) as the raw input for the third token, the model will output `B'. During the training phase, the model actually produces (A, C, B), representing an over-correction. Alternatively, during inference, (A, C) will not be corrected, allowing the model to produce a reasonable output such as (A, C, C). 

