      \field{abstract}{A simple model with a novel type of dynamics is introduced in order to investigate the emergence of self-ordered motion in systems of particles with biologically motivated interaction. In our model particles are driven with a constant absolute velocity and at each time step assume the average direction of motion of the particles in their neighborhood with some random perturbation ({$H$}) added. We present numerical evidence that this model results in a kinetic phase transition from no transport (zero average velocity, {|}{$\mathbf{v}a\vert$} = 0) to finite net transport through spontaneous symmetry breaking of the rotational symmetry. The transition is continuous, since {|}{$\mathbf{v}a\vert$} is found to scale as ({$Hc-H$}){$B$} with {$B\simeq$}0.45.}
