\section{Spectrum of Laplace operator}

\label{app:laplace}

Assume that $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{3}$ stands for the open unit ball in
$\mathbb{R}^{3}$ and consider the eigenvalue equation for the Laplace
operator
\begin{equation}%
\begin{cases}
-\triangle u(x)=\lambda^{2}u,\quad x\in\Omega\\
\left.  \frac{\partial u}{\partial n}\right\vert _{\partial\Omega}=0.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:Lap-spec1}%
\end{equation}
In the case $\lambda=0$ we know that the only solutions are the constant
functions. In order to describe the spectrum of $-\triangle$ for $\lambda
^{2}>0$, one can use the method of separation of variables using the spherical
coordinates $(r,\theta)$ in $\mathbb{R}^{3}$ (here $\theta\in S^{2}$). To be
more precise, the Laplace operator $-\triangle$ in spherical coordinates can
be written as
\begin{equation}
\triangle u=\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial r^{2}}+\frac{2}{r}\frac{\partial
u}{\partial r}+\frac{1}{r^{2}}\triangle_{S^{2}}u, \label{eq:L-B}%
\end{equation}
where $\triangle_{S^{2}}$ is the Laplace--Beltrami operator on $S^{2}$
($\triangle_{S^{2}}$ is also called the \textit{spherical Laplacian}).

In order to find
the non-zero solutions to \eqref{eq:Lap-spec1}, we consider a particular form of functions $u$,
namely $u(r,\bm\theta)=R(r)\cdot T(\bm\theta)$ ($\bm\theta\in S^{2}$), which
substituted to the equation $-\triangle u=\lambda^{2}u$ leads to
\[
\left(  -R^{\prime\prime}-\frac{2}{r}R^{\prime}\right)  T-\frac{1}{r^{2}
}\left(  \triangle_{S^{2}}(T)\right)  R=\lambda^{2}RT
\]
that can be written as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sep-Lap}\frac{r^{2}R^{\prime\prime}+2rR^{\prime}}{R}+\lambda
^{2}r^{2}=-\frac{\triangle_{S2}(T)}{T}.
\end{equation}
Since the functions in \eqref{eq:sep-Lap} standing on the left and on the
right hand side of equation depend on different variables, there must exist a
constant $c$ such that
\begin{align}
r^{2}R^{\prime\prime}+2rR^{\prime}+(\lambda^{2}r^{2}-c)R  &
=0,\label{eq:bessel}\\
-\triangle_{S^{2}}(T)  &  =cT. \label{eq:spherilcal-L}%
\end{align}
The equation \eqref{eq:spherilcal-L} implies that $c$ is an eigenvalue of the
spherical Laplacian $-\triangle_{S^{2}}$.

By using the spherical coordinates $(\theta,\varphi)$, $\theta\in\lbrack
0,2\pi]$, $\varphi\in\lbrack0,\pi]$, we obtain the following formula for the
Laplacian $\triangle_{S^{2}}$,
\[
\triangle_{S^{2}}=\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial\varphi^{2}}+\frac{\cos\varphi
}{\sin\varphi}\frac{\partial}{\partial\varphi}+\frac{1}{\sin^{2}\varphi}
\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial\theta^{2}}.
\]
Then, by substituting $T(\theta,\varphi)=\Theta(\theta)\Phi(\varphi)$ to the
equation $-\triangle_{S^{2}}T=cT$, we obtain
\begin{equation}
\Theta\Phi^{\prime\prime}+\frac{\cos\varphi}{\sin\varphi}\Theta\Phi^{\prime
}+\frac{1}{\sin^{2}\varphi}\Theta^{\prime\prime}\Phi=-c\Theta\Phi.
\label{eq:S2-L1}%
\end{equation}
Then, by separating the variables in \eqref{eq:S2-L1}, we obtain
\[
\frac{\sin^{2}\varphi\Phi^{\prime\prime}+\cos\varphi\sin\varphi\Phi^{\prime}
}{\Phi}+c\sin^{2}\varphi=-\frac{\Theta^{\prime\prime}}{\Theta}=n^{2},
\]
where $n=0,1,\dots$. Then, clearly $\Theta(\theta)=a\cos(n\theta
)+b\sin(n\theta)$ and therefore
\begin{equation}
\sin^{2}\varphi\Phi^{\prime\prime}+\cos\varphi\sin\varphi\Phi^{\prime
}+\Big(c\sin^{2}\varphi-n^{2}\Big)\Phi=0. \label{eq:Legendre}%
\end{equation}
The equation \eqref{eq:Legendre}, by using the change of variables
$s=\cos\varphi$, and $P(s)=P(\cos\varphi)=\Phi(\phi)$, is transformed into the
the classical Legendre equation
\begin{equation}
(1-s^{2})P^{\prime\prime}-2sP^{\prime}+\left(  c-\frac{n^{2}}{1-s^{2}}\right)
P=0,
\end{equation}
which, for $c=k(k+1)$ with $k\in\mathbb{Z}$, admits a bounded solution
$P_{k}^{n}$, the so-called called \textit{Legendre function}) on $[-1,1]$. One
can use the following well-known formula for $P_{k}^{n}$
\[
P_{k}^{n}(s)=\frac{(1-s^{2})^{\frac{n}{2}}}{2^{k}k!}\frac{d^{k+n}}{ds^{k+n}
}(s^{2}-1)^{k},
\]
to conclude that $P_{k}^{n}\equiv0$ for $n>k$, i.e. for $n=1,2,3,\dots$, and
$k\geq n$, $P_{k}^{n}$ is a non-constant solution to \eqref{eq:Legendre}
satisfying the boundary condition $P(-1)=P(1)=0$.

Consequently, we obtain that the eigenfunctions of $-\triangle_{S^{2}}$,
corresponding to the eigenvalue $c=k(k+1)$, $k\geq0$, are
\[
T(\theta,\varphi):=P(\cos\varphi)\Big(a\cos(n\theta)+b\sin(n\theta)\Big)\quad
a,\,b\in\mathbb{R},\;0\leq n\leq k,
\]
where $P$ is a solution to \eqref{eq:Legendre}. Put
\[
T_{k}^{n}(\theta,\varphi):=P_{k}^{n}(\cos\varphi)\Big(a\cos(n\theta
)+b\sin(n\theta)\Big)\quad a,\,b\in\mathbb{R},\;0\leq n\leq k.
\]
The function $T_{k}^{n}$ are called \textit{surface harmonics} of degree $k$.
Then the eigenspace $\mathbb{V}_{k}$ of $-\triangle_{S^{2}}$ (considered as an
unbounded operator in $L^{2}(S^{2},\mathbb{R})$) is generated by the functions
$T_{k}^{n}$, i.e.
\[
\mathbb{V}_{k}=\text{span}\{T_{k}^{n}:0\leq n\leq k\}.
\]
The orthogonality properties of Legendre functions imply that
\[
\text{dim}\mathbb{V}_{k}=1+2k.
\]


Then, by using that $c=k(k+1)$ and the substitution $R(r):=r^{-1/2}\hat{R}
(r)$, equation \eqref{eq:bessel} becomes
\begin{equation}
r^{2}\hat{R}^{\prime\prime}+r\hat{R}^{\prime2}+\left(  \lambda^{2}
r^{2}-\left(  k+1/2\right)  ^{2}\right)  \hat{R}=0. \label{eq:bessel1}%
\end{equation}
If $\lambda\neq0$, after changing variable using $\widetilde{R}(t)=\hat
{R}(r/\lambda)$, \eqref{eq:bessel1} becomes the classical Bessel equation
\[
t^{2}\widetilde{R}^{\prime\prime}(t)+t\widetilde{R}^{\prime}(t)+\left(
t^{2}-\left(  k+1/2\right)  ^{2}\right)  \widetilde{R}(t)=0.
\]
The bounded solution at zero are $\widetilde{R}(t)=J_{k+1/2}(t)$ , where
$J_{k+1/2}$ stands for the $k+1/2$-th Bessel function of the first kind.
Therefore, we obtain that solutions to \eqref{eq:bessel} are constant
multiples of $R(r)=r^{-1/2}J_{k+1/2}(\lambda r)$ (notice that $R(r)$ is finite
at zero). Consequently, $u(r,\theta)=R(r)\Theta(\bm\theta)$ satisfies the
Neumann condition if
\[
R^{\prime}(1)=\lambda J_{k+1/2}^{\prime}(\lambda)-\frac{1}{2}J_{k+1/2}
(\lambda)=0.
\]
In this way, we obtain that the spectrum of $\mathscr L$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\sigma(\mathscr L)=\left\{  s_{km}:k,m\in\mathbb{N}\right\}  . \label{eq:spL}%
\end{equation}
where $s_{00}=0$ and in other cases $s_{km}$ is the $m$-th positive zero of
$\lambda J_{k+1/2}^{\prime}(\lambda)-\frac{1}{2}J_{k+1/2}(\lambda)$. Moreover,
we have the following subspace $\mathcal{E}_{km}$ with eigenvalue $s_{km}$:
$\mathcal{E}_{00}=\left\langle 1\right\rangle $ and
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}_{km}=\left\langle r^{-\frac{1}{2}}J_{k+1/2}(s_{km}r)P_{k}^{n}
(\cos\varphi)\Big(a\cos(n\theta)+b\sin(n\theta)\Big):a,\,b\in\mathbb{R},0\leq
n\leq k\right\rangle . \label{eigenspace}%
\end{equation}
Notice that the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $s_{km}$ is the
subspace generated by $\left\{  \mathcal{E}_{jn}:s_{jn}=s_{km}\right\}  $, and
in the case that $s_{km}$ is isotopic simple the corresponding eigenspace is
$\mathcal{E}_{km}$.

\section{Conjugacy classes of $O(3)$ and $S_4^p$}
\subsection{Conjugacy classes of $O(3)$}
\label{sec:notation} The subgroups of $O(3)=SO(3)\times{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$,
classified up to conjugacy, are\newline(i) the product subgroups of type
$H\times{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$, where $H\leq SO(3)$;\newline(ii) the twisted
subgroups of $SO(3)\times{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$, which are
\[
H^{\varphi}:=\{(g,z)\in SO(3)\times{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}:\varphi(g)=z\},
\]
where $H\leq SO(3)$ and $\varphi:H\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$ is a
homomorphism. In particular, the subgroups $H$ of $SO(3)$, which are $SO(3)$,
$O(2)$, $SO(2)$, $D_{n}$, $n\geq2$ ($D_{2}$ denoted by $V_{4}$ is the {Klein
group}), ${\mathbb{Z}}_{n}$, $n\geq1$, and the exceptional groups $A_{4}$ --
the {tetrahedral group}, $S_{4}$ -- the {octahedral group} and $A_{5}$ --
the {icosahedral group}, can be identified with $H^{\varphi}$ where
$\varphi:H\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$ is a trivial homomorphism. We also
denote by $z:D_{n}\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$ the epimorphism satisfying
$\ker(z)={\mathbb{Z}}_{n}$, by $d:D_{2n}\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$, the
epimorphism with $\ker(d)=D_{n}$; $d:{\mathbb{Z}}_{2n}\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}
}_{2}$ with $\ker(d)={\mathbb{Z}}_{n}$; and we denote by symbol ${\ -}$ the
epimorphism $\varphi:S_{4}\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$ with $\ker
(\varphi)=A_{4}$; similarly, we denote by $-$ the epimorphisms $\varphi
:V_{4}\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$ with $\ker(\varphi)={\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$,
$\varphi:O(2)\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$ with $\ker(\varphi)=SO(2)$ and
$\varphi:O(3)\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}_{2}$ with $\ker(\varphi)=SO(3)$.
