\subsection{$G$-Equivariant Gradient Degree in Hilbert Spaces}

Let $\mathscr H$ be a Hilbert space and consider a $C^{1}$-differentiable
$G$-invariant functional $f:\mathscr{H}\to\mathbb{R}$ given by $f(x)=\frac
12\|x\|^{2}-\varphi(x)$, $x\in\mathscr{H}$. Then,
\[
\nabla f(x)=x-\nabla\varphi(x),\quad x\in\mathscr{H}.
\]
We say that the functional $\varphi$ is admissible if $\nabla\varphi
:\mathscr{H}\to\mathscr{H}$ is a completely continuous map. Suppose
$\Omega\subset\mathscr{H}$ is a $G$-invariant bounded open set such that the
map $\nabla f:\mathscr{H}\to\mathscr{H}$ is $\Omega$-admissible, i.e.,
\[
\forall_{x\in\partial\Omega}\;\;\;\nabla f(x)=x-\nabla\varphi(x)\not =0.
\]
By a \textit{$G$-equivariant approximation scheme} $\{P_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$
in $\mathscr{H}$, we mean a sequence of $G$-equivariant orthogonal projections
$P_{n}:\mathscr{H}\to\mathscr{H}$, $n=1,2,\dots$, such that:

\begin{enumerate}
%\item \renewcommand\labelenumi{(\alph{enumi})}


\item the subspaces $\mathscr{H}^{n}:=P_{n}(\mathscr{H})$, $n=1,2,\dots$, are
finite dimensional;

\item $\mathscr{H}^{n}\subset\mathscr{H}^{n+1}$, $n=0,1,2,\dots$;

\item $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}P_{n}x=x$ for all $x\in\mathscr{H}$.
\end{enumerate}

\vskip.3cm Consider a $G$-equivariant $C^{1}$-differentiable admissible
functional $\varphi:\mathscr{H}\to\mathbb{R}$ and let $\Omega\subset
\mathscr{H}$ be a $G$-invariant bounded open set such that $\nabla f$ is
$\Omega$-admissible, where $f(x):=\frac12\|x\|^{2}-\varphi(x)$, $x\in
\mathscr{H}$. In order to define the $G$-equivariant gradient degree
$\nabla_{G}\mbox{\rm -}\deg(\nabla f,\Omega)$, we consider a $G$-equivariant
approximation scheme $\{P_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ in $\mathscr{H}$. Define
$f_{n}:\mathscr{H}_{n}\to\mathbb{R}$ by $f_{n}(x):=\frac12\|x\|^{2}
-\varphi(x)$, $x\in\mathscr{H}_{n}$. Then, $\nabla f_{n}:\mathscr{H}_{n}
\to\mathscr{H}_{n}$ is $\Omega_{n}$-admissible for $n$ sufficiently large,
where $\Omega_{n}:=\Omega\cap\mathscr{H}_{n}$, and therefore the
$G$-equivariant degrees $\nabla_{G}\mbox{\rm -}\deg(\nabla f_{n},\Omega_{n})$
are well-defined for $n$ sufficiently large. On the other hand, by the
suspension property of the $G $-equivariant gradient degree, for sufficiently
large $n$, we have
\[
\nabla_{G}\mbox{\rm -}\deg(\nabla f_{n},\Omega_{n})= \nabla_{G}
\mbox{\rm -}\deg(\nabla f_{n+1},\Omega_{n+1})
\]
which implies that
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:grad-H}\nabla_{G}\mbox{\rm -}\deg(\nabla f,\Omega):= \nabla
_{G}\mbox{\rm -}\deg(\nabla f_{n},\Omega_{n}),\;\; \mbox{where}\;n\;\text{is
sufficiently large}.
\end{equation}
One can verify that this construction does not depend on the choice of a
$G$-approximation scheme in the space $\mathscr{H}$. \vskip.3cm The
$G$-equivariant gradient degree defined by \eqref{eq:grad-H} has all the
standard properties of a topological degree, i.e., existence, additivity,
homotopy and multiplicativity.

