\label{thm:path-star}
If $m\ge 2$ and $n\ge 3$, then $\pch(P_m, K_{1,n}) = 3$ and $\pchu(P_m, K_{1,n}) \le 9$. Moreover, if  $n=3$ and $m\ge 10$, then $\pchu(P_m, K_{1,n}) \in \{6,7,8,9\}$, and if $n>3$ and $m\ge 35$, then $\pchu(P_m, K_{1,n}) \in \{7,8,9\}$.
