 \begin{center}
  Abstract  
\end{center} 
}
We present a comprehensive comparison of the physics sensitivities of a Liquid Argon Time Projection Chamber (LArTPC) and a Water-based Liquid Scintillator (WbLS) detector, considering their potential deployment as the fourth far detector module in the DUNE facility. 
Using GLoBES-based simulations, we evaluate their performance in measuring standard neutrino oscillation parameters ($\theta_{23}, \delta_{13}$ and $\Delta m^{2}_{31}$), both in standard 3-neutrino case, as well as in presence of  new physics scenarios involving light sterile neutrinos and neutral-current non-standard interactions (NC NSI). 
Our findings show that THEIA (a WbLS-based detector) significantly outperforms LArTPC in resolving the CP phase $\delta_{13}$,- especially near maximal CP violation, and in lifting the octant degeneracy of $\theta_{23}$ due to its superior energy resolution and ability to clearly identify the second oscillation maximum. 
Furthermore, THEIA offers competitive reconstruction precision even with relatively moderate energy resolutions ($7-10\%/\sqrt{E}$) and demonstrates enhanced robustness under new physics scenarios. These results support the physics-driven case for a hybrid DUNE configuration utilizing both LArTPC and WbLS technologies for optimized sensitivity across the full spectrum of neutrino oscillation and physics beyond the standard model.

\vspace*{.5cm}

\end{titlepage}


%============================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
%==============================
Despite the remarkable success of the three-flavor neutrino oscillation framework~\cite{10.5281/zenodo.4726908, 
deSalas:2020pgw, nufit_globalfit, Esteban:2024eli, Capozzi:2025wyn}, several key questions remain open. 
Chief among these is whether CP violation occurs in the lepton sector, encoded in the phase $\delta_{\mathrm{13}}$, whose value remains largely unconstrained. 
The precise octant of the atmospheric mixing angle $\theta_{23}$, whether it lies below or above $45^\circ$, is also yet to be resolved. 
Furthermore, the neutrino mass ordering,- whether neutrinos follow a normal or inverted hierarchy,-remains undetermined. 
These ambiguities lead to parameter degeneracies that can only be resolved through high-statistics, high-resolution measurements across multiple oscillation channels and energy spectra. Additionally, the possibility of deviations from the standard three-flavor paradigm due to possible new physics scenarios such as the presence of light ($\sim$ eV) sterile neutrinos (as hinted by long-standing low energy event excess anomalies in LSND~\cite{Hill:1995gf,LSND:1996ubh,LSND:1997vun,LSND:2001aii}, MiniBooNE~\cite{MiniBooNE:2013uba, MiniBooNE:2020pnu, Arguelles:2021meu} and Gallium experiments\cite{GALLEX:1997lja, SAGE:1998fvr, Kaether:2010ag, Giunti:2010zu, Barinov:2021asz}) or Non-standard interaction (NSI)~\cite{Wolfenstein:1977ue, Barger1991, Grossman1995, Ohlsson2013} introduce further complexity, necessitating diverse detector capabilities. 

The \textit{Deep Underground Neutrino Experiment} (DUNE)~\cite{Acciarri:2015uup, DUNE:2016rla} is poised to be a transformative facility in the exploration of neutrino properties and the search for new physics. 
Beyond these goals, DUNE's experimental versatility and precision reach enable it to probe potential physics beyond the Standard Model (BSM), such as non-standard interactions (NSI) and the presence of light sterile neutrinos. 
A critical component in realizing this broad physics agenda lies in the detector technology employed at the far site.

The current DUNE design includes four far detector (FD) modules, with evolving strategies for each~\cite{mary_dune_theia_2023, DUNE:2024wvj}. 
Module 1 is a finalized single-phase Liquid Argon Time Projection Chamber (LArTPC) currently under construction. 
Module 2, originally proposed as a dual-phase LArTPC, is being reconsidered due to technical and schedule risks, possibly shifting to an improved single-phase design. 
Module 3 is under active R\&D, exploring vertical drift and other advanced LArTPC variants. Module 4 remains undefined by design, to accommodate innovative technologies that may offer complementary capabilities.

Among the leading candidates for the fourth module is the THEIA detector, a water-based liquid scintillator (WbLS) design~\cite{OrebiGann:2015gus, Fischer:2018zsr, Theia:2019non, Kaptanoglu:2019gtg, mary_dune_theia_2023, Callaghan:2023oyu}. 
THEIA offers several unique advantages over traditional LArTPCs and pure scintillator detectors. 
Its core innovation lies in its ability to separate Cherenkov and scintillation light, using fast photodetectors and chromatic photon sorting techniques. 
This enables the detector to retain both excellent energy resolution (from scintillation) and directional information (from Cherenkov light),- a powerful combination not available in conventional  LArTPC or scintillator detectors. 
Directionality is particularly critical for identifying the incoming neutrino flavor and distinguishing backgrounds. 
The superior energy resolution can help THEIA to identify the oscillation patterns and their possible small deviations more precisely. 
The advantages of THEIA in neutrino oscillation physics has been demonstrated in literature, 
for \eg, in improving CP violation sensitivities beyond DUNE~\cite{Ge:2022iac} and in 
efficient detection of antineutrinos by leveraging the directional capability of THEIA~\cite{Zsoldos:2022mre}. 

In addition, WbLS technology supports very low detection thresholds, making THEIA capable of accessing physics across a wide energy range, including MeV-scale neutrinos from astrophysical origins. 
All these capabilities also enhance sensitivity to BSM physics, where subtle spectral or angular distortions may occur. 
For instance, such low detection threshold of THEIA can help in the search for Diffused Supernova 
Neutrino Background (DSNB)~\cite{DeGouvea:2020ang, Sawatzki:2020mpb}, neutrinos from primordial black hole (PBH)~\cite{DeRomeri:2021xgy}, and also in multimessenger astronomy as a tool for gravitational wave-neutrino correlations and rare process serches~\cite{Borboruah:2025hai}. 
Various capabilities of THEIA in probing physics beyond the standard model (BSM) has also been discussed in literature,-in probing neutrino decay~\cite{DeGouvea:2020ang}, 
absolute neutrino mass~\cite{Parker:2023cos} and in studying the strange quark content in nucleons~\cite{Chauhan:2022wgj}. 
 For a summary of the physics program of THEIA, including oscillation physics, astroparticle physics, as well as probing BSM physics, the readers may 
look at Ref. \cite{Theia:2022uyh}.

In this paper, we present a detailed comparative study of the physics potential of a LArTPC and a WbLS-based detector, assuming their deployment as the fourth FD module in DUNE. 
We evaluate and contrast their capabilities in terms of event spectra, asymmetries, parameter exclusion, and reconstructed confidence contours in the space of standard three-flavor oscillation parameters. Specifically, we focus on the sensitivity and reconstruction of $\delta_{\mathrm{CP}}$, $\theta_{23}$, and $\Delta m^2_{31}$, and quantify the performance using $\chisq$ analyses and parameter uncertainties.
Furthermore, we investigate how each detector configuration responds to the inclusion of a single new physics scenario,- either a light sterile neutrino or neutral current (NC) NSI. 
These scenarios introduce additional oscillation parameters and degeneracies, which can be mitigated or amplified depending on the detector's reconstruction capabilities. 
We analyze the resulting modifications in the sensitivity and precision of standard parameters in the presence of such BSM effects.
Unless otherwise mentioned explicitly, we have considered normal mass hierarchy (NH). 



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The article is organized as follows. 
We start with a discussion of the experimental configurations of DUNE and THEIA in Sec.\ \ref{sec:simulation}. 
We then discuss the oscillation probabilities and the event spectra at DUNE and THEIA configurations in Sec.\ \ref{sec:prob_event}.
We follow it up in Sec.\ \ref{sec:asym} with a discussion about CP asymmetries at both probability and event level. 
We show our statistical results in Sec.\ \ref{sec:contour} by analyzing the capabilities of DUNE and THEIA to exclude parameter space associated to $\tc, \da$ and $\ldm$. 
Finally, in Sec.\ \ref{sec:precision}, we compare DUNE and THEIA in their abilities to reconstruct the oscillation parameters and quantify the precision with which the reconstruction can be done. 
We summarise our results in Sec.\ \ref{sec:summary}.


%============================
\section{Simulation Details}
\label{sec:simulation}
%===============================
DUNE is an upcoming long baseline neutrino experiment based in the USA. 
It has a baseline of approximately 1300 km from the neutrino source at Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory (FNAL), in Batavia, Illinois to the 
FD having a fiducial mass of 40 kiloton (kt) and located at South Dakota. 
The experiment is capable of using a proton beam of power 1.2 MW and of running 6.5 years each on $\nu$ and 
$\bar{\nu}$ mode (resulting in a total exposure of roughly 624 kt.MW.yr corresponding to total 
$1.1 \times 10^{21}$ protons on target or POT per year). 
See the Technical Design Report (TDR)~\cite{DUNE:2020ypp} by the DUNE collaboration 
for more details. 
We use the General Long Baseline Experiment Simulator (GLoBES)~\cite{Huber:2004ka, Huber:2007ji} for simulating DUNE, 
using the latest GLoBES configuration files (including the flux, cross-sections, migration matrices for energy reconstruction, efficiencies \etc ) corresponding to the TDR by the DUNE collaboration~\cite{DUNE:2021cuw}. 
This analysis was performed at DUNE FD, after taking into account the rate-based constraints at the near detector (ND), without explicitly simulating the ND~\cite{DUNE:2020fgq}.   
Originally, the DUNE FD complex is supposed to have four LArTPC  modules, - each having a fiducial mass of 10 kt. 
The LArTPC technology provides millimeter-scale 3D tracking and calorimetry through ionization signals created by charged particles traversing the liquid argon volume. 
The standard energy resolution for electromagnetic showers is approximately $15\%/\sqrt{E}$, and for hadronic showers, it is roughly $30\%/\sqrt{E}$~\cite{DUNE:2020lwj}~\footnote{In the energy range of $0.5-4$ GeV which is relevant for oscillation measurements, the migration matrices used in the GLoBES simulation for DUNE provides an energy resolution of $15\%-20\%/\sqrt{E}$ for the reconstructed neutrino events~\cite{DUNE:2021cuw}.}.
The neutrino event selection efficiencies for LArTPC, taken from Ref.\ \cite{DUNE:2021cuw} are estimated by training an  
image recognition technique named convolutional visual network on Monte Carlo simulations of neutrino interactions~\cite{Aurisano:2016jvx, Abi:2020qib}. 
The efficiencies for appearance events vary from around $70\%$ around 0.5 GeV to close to $90\%$ 
around 2-3 GeV. 
The appearance efficiency starts decreasing after 4-5 GeV and become $70\%$ or lower around energies greater than 7-8 GeV. 
The disappearance efficiencies are approximately $60\%$ around 0.5 GeV and increase to $80-85\%$ around 3 GeV and remain flat thereafter. 
However, DUNE's LArTPCs have limited directional capability for lower-energy events. Directionality can only be indirectly inferred from track geometry or topology and becomes increasingly difficult below a few hundred MeV.

As mentioned in the introduction, the detection technology of the module 4 of the FD complex 
is still under discussion, and a WbLS detector (called THEIA) instead of an LArTPC is a leading candidate for it. 
THEIA merges the directional information of Cherenkov light with the high light yield and low threshold of a scintillator detector. 
The baseline design~\cite{Theia:2019non} incorporates fast-timing photon detectors (\eg, Large Area Picosecond Photodetectors or LAPPDs), chromatic photon separation (\eg, via dichroicons), and a scalable modular geometry.

THEIA operates with both Cherenkov and scintillation light, allowing it to reconstruct both the energy and direction of charged particles. 
The separation of these two light components enables excellent particle identification, crucial for distinguishing $\nu_e$, $\nu_\mu$, and $\nu_\tau$ interactions and suppressing neutral current backgrounds. 
According to performance studies in Ref.\ \cite{Theia:2019non} and follow-ups, THEIA is expected to achieve an unprecedented level of energy resolution,- as low as approximately $3\%/\sqrt{E}$ for GeV-scale events and sub-percent resolution at MeV scales.
In addition, THEIA's low detection threshold (down to $\sim$1 MeV) makes it uniquely suited for  detection of diffuse supernova neutrino background (DSNB), solar neutrinos, and other low-energy physics such as geo-neutrinos, neutrinoless double beta decay or nucleon decay,- regimes less accessible to LArTPCs. 

In our GLoBES simulation, we follow the description of the detector THEIA-25 (described in Ref.\ \cite{Theia:2019non}) consisting of a solution of $5\%$ liquid scintillator in water. 
The detector features  $90\%$ optical coverage with $25^{\circ}$ angular resolution, and can potentially be installed in the cavern designated for the module 4 of DUNE FD. 
The detector performance is assumed to be similar to that of a conventional water Cherenkov detector, based on Monte Carlo simulations from Super-Kamiokande (SK)~\cite{Super-Kamiokande:2002weg}.
A constant selection efficiency of $51\%$ is applied along the entire energy range~\cite{lee_thesis_theia_2023}.
The fiducial mass of THEIA-25 is taken to be 17 kt, consistent with studies indicating that it yields CP violation sensitivities comparable to those of a 10 kt (fiducial) LArTPC DUNE FD~\cite{Acciarri:2015uup, Theia:2019non}. 

In what follows, we compare the physics sensitivities of two sets of different detector configurations at the DUNE FD site:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{DUNE:} Here we use a 10 kt (fiducial) LArTPC acting as one DUNE FD module. 
The GLoBES configurations (efficiencies, migration matrices, signal/background considerations \etc\ ) of the setup is based on Ref.\ \cite{DUNE:2021cuw}. We refer to this setup simply as DUNE hereafter. 

\item \textit{THEIA:} Here we use a 17 kt (fiducial) WbLS as one of the DUNE FD module. 
The GLoBES configurations are based on a standard water cherenkov detector like SK, and 
taken from Refs.\ \cite{Theia:2019non, lee_thesis_theia_2023}. 
We consider three different Gaussian energy resolutions, namely $3\%/\sqrt{E}, 7\%/\sqrt{E}, 10\%/\sqrt{E}$ using the bin-based automatic energy smearing method described in GLoBES manual~\cite{globes_manual_2020}. This setup is dubbed as THEIA hereafter.  
\end{itemize} 

