\section{Oscillation probability and event spectra}
\label{sec:prob_event}
%===============================
A wide band beam experiment like DUNE is expected to collect 
oscillation data over a wide range of $L/E$, - potentially 
observing more than one oscillation cycles. 
It has been shown in literature how multiple oscillation cycles at DUNE  
can aid in resolving the standard unknown issues such as CP violation, mass ordering, octant sensitivities~\cite{Huber:2010dx, Qian:2013nhp, Ghosh:2014rna, Rout:2020emr}, as well as in
probing new physics~\cite{Choubey:2020dhw, Chakraborty:2020cfu}.
In order to understand the locations of the oscillation peaks and dips, we first analyze the probability expression in vacuum  for the $\mue$ channel~\cite{Akhmedov:2004ny}.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{align}\label{eq:prob}
 P_{\mu e} &\simeq \sin^{2}2\theta_{13}\sin^{2}\theta_{23}\sin^{2}\Delta_{31} 
 +
  \alpha \Delta_{31} \sin2\theta_{13} \sin2\theta_{23}  \sin 2\theta_{12}  
 \sin \Delta_{31} \cos (\da + \Delta_{31}) \nonumber \\
 &= \sin\Delta_{31} \big[\sin^{2}2\theta_{13}\sin^{2}\theta_{23}\sin \Delta_{31} 
 +
  \alpha \Delta_{31} \sin2\theta_{13} \sin2\theta_{23}  \sin 2\theta_{12}  
  \cos (\da + \Delta_{31})  \big],
 \end{align}
 %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%\begin{align}\label{eq:prob}
% P_{\mu e} &\simeq \sin^{2}2\theta_{13}\sin^{2}\theta_{23}\frac{\sin^{2}(1-\hat{A})\Delta_{31}}{(1-\hat{A})^{2}} \nonumber \\
% &+ \alpha^{2} \sin^{2}2\theta_{12} \cos^{2}\theta_{23} \frac{\sin^{2}\hat{A}\Delta_{31}}{\hat{A}^{2}} \nonumber \\
% &+ \alpha \sin2\theta_{13} \sin2\theta_{23}  \sin 2\theta_{12}  
% \frac{\sin (1-\hat{A})\Delta_{31}}{(1-\hat{A})} \frac{\sin \hat{A}\Delta_{31}}{\hat{A}} \nonumber \\
% &\times \big(\cos \Delta_{31} \cos \Delta -  \sin \delta \sin \Delta\big),
% \end{align}
 where $\alpha = \frac{\sdm}{\ldm}$ and $\Delta_{31} = \frac{\ldm L}{4E}$. 
 %and $\hat{A} = \frac{2\sqrt{2}G_{F}N_{e}E}{\ldm}$ is the matter effect. 
 Note that, here we neglect the {\it{solar}} term 
$\alpha^{2} \sin^{2} 2\theta_{12} c_{23}^{2} \frac{\sin^{2}\hat{A}\Delta_{31}}{\hat{A}^{2}}$ since it is roughly 3-4 orders of magnitude smaller than the terms shown.
From Eq.\ \ref{eq:prob}, the oscillation maximum for $\pmue$  happens  when the overall factor $\sin \Delta_{31}$ is 1 (Note that this will also maximise the leading term $\sin^{2}2\theta_{13}\sin^{2}\theta_{23} \sin \Delta_{31}$ inside the square bracket in Eq.\ \ref{eq:prob}).  
 After properly taking into account the units, this implies, 
 %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
 \begin{align}\label{eq:osc_max}
 \Delta_{31} = 1.27 \times \frac{\ldm [\text{eV}^{2}] L [\text{km}]}{E_{n}^{\text{OM}} [\text{GeV}]} \simeq (2n-1) \frac{\pi}{2}  \text{\qquad}(n = 1, 2..).
 \end{align}
 %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
$n = 1, 2$ denote the first and second oscillation maxima (OM) respectively (hereafter dubbed as $\om{1}$ and $\om{2}$ respectively). 
For a baseline (L) of 1300 km, the corresponding energies where the maxima occur are,
$E_{1}^{\text{OM}} \simeq 2.5 \text{ GeV, } E_{2}^{\text{OM}} = E_{1}^{\text{OM}}/3 \simeq 0.8$ GeV.

Eq.\ \ref{eq:prob} also tells us that $\pmue$ has a dip or minimum when the overall factor $\sin \Delta_{31}$ is zero at some energy $E_{n}$. 
This implies the condition,
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
 \begin{align}\label{eq:osc_min}
 \Delta_{31} = 1.27 \times \frac{\ldm [\text{eV}^{2}] L [\text{km}]}{E_{n} [\text{GeV}]} \simeq n\pi  \text{\qquad}(n = 1, 2..).
 \end{align}
 %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Note that, $n$ cannot be zero since it would imply zero propagation length. 
In vacuum, for $\ldm = 2.5 \times 10^{-3} \text{ eV}^{2}$ and $L = 1300$ km, such an oscillation minimum occurs (for $n=1$) around 1.3 GeV.  

