\section{CP asymmetry}
\label{sec:asym}
%===============================
The difference between the oscillation channel $\mue$ and its conjugate $\muebar$ encodes information about leptonic CP phase $\da$. 
From Eq.\ \ref{eq:prob}, the CP asymmetry defined as the probability difference between these two channels in vacuum is given by,
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:diff}
   \acp = \pmue-\pmuebar \simeq 
 -2\alpha \sin2\theta_{13} \sin2\theta_{23} \sin2\theta_{12} \Delta_{31} \sin^{2}\Delta_{31} \sin\da. 
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
An observation of difference between the $\mue$ and $\muebar$ 
transitions can thus demonstrate leptonic CP violation \ie, whether $\da \neq 0,\pm\pi$~\cite{Marciano:2006uc, LBNE:2013dhi}. 
However, from Eqs.\ \ref{eq:osc_max} and \ref{eq:diff} we note that the magnitude of the CP asymmetry $\acp$ is three times at the second oscillation maximum ($\Delta_{31} \simeq 3\pi/2$ at $\om{2}$) than that at the first ($\Delta_{31} \simeq \pi/2$ at $\om{1}$)~\footnote{It is worth noting that Earth matter effects,- which increase with energy,- enhance the observed asymmetry, making the CP violation sensitivity at the first oscillation maximum ($\om{1}$) comparable to that at the second ($\om{2}$). 
However, the intrinsic CP violation, governed solely by the phase $\da$, must be disentangled from matter-induced asymmetries, which can mimic but do not constitute genuine CP-violating effects~\cite{Arafune:1997hd, Nunokawa:2007qh, Ohlsson:2013ip, Rout:2017udo}.}. 
Hence $\om{2}$ potentially offers more sensitivity to probe CP violation~\cite{Diwan:2003bp}.


