We quantify a measure of CP violation by analyzing the corresponding 
event asymmetry as the difference between the conjugate event spectra: $\ncp = (\nmue - \nmuebar)$.
In Fig.\ \ref{fig:asym}, we plot $\ncp$ for 
DUNE as well as for the four configurations of THEIA. 
$\ncp$ is maximum around $2-3$ GeV approximately. 
Note that, the number of events around $\om{2}$ ($\simeq 0.8$ GeV) is small. This is due to the fact that the fluxes to be used at DUNE are optimized to peak near $\om{1}$ of the probability. 

In order to take into account the lack of knowledge of the CP phase $\da$, we now proceed to analyze the heatplots for   $\acp$ and $\ncp$ in Figs.\ \ref{fig:heatplot_acp} and \ref{fig:heatplot_ncp} respectively, by varying both $\da$ and energy E. 
Fig.\ \ref{fig:heatplot_acp} shows the standard 3-neutrino case, as well as, demonstrates the scenario when a possible new physics may be present in nature. 

We consider two possible well-motivated new physics relevant for DUNE~\cite{DUNE:2020fgq},- namely the presence of a eV-scale sterile neutrino, and the presence of Neutral Current (NC) Non-standard interaction (NSI).
In presence of a sterile neutrino, after choosing a specific parameterization~\cite{Gandhi:2015xza, Gandhi:2017vzo, Parveen:2024bcc} there are three additional mixing angles ($\theta_{14}, \theta_{24}, \theta_{34}$); two more CP phases ($\delta_{24}, \delta_{34}$) and one additional mass-squared difference $\Delta m^{2}_{41}$. 
The values of the sterile parameters used in our study are listed in table \ref{tab:parameters}.
NC NSI describes the interaction between the neutrinos and the first generation fermions, and the relevant hamiltonian affecting neutirno propagation in presence of NC NSI can be written as~\cite{Wolfenstein:1977ue, Barger1991, Grossman1995, Ohlsson2013}:
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H} = \dfrac{1}{2 E} \Bigg[U\left(
\begin{array}{ccc}
0   &  &  \\  &  \Delta m^2_{21} &   \\ 
 &  & \Delta m^2_{31} \\
\end{array} 
\right) U^\dagger 
 + A   \left(
\begin{array}{ccc}
1+ \varepsilon_{ee}  & \varepsilon_{e \mu}  & 
\varepsilon_{e \tau}  \\ {\varepsilon_{e\mu} }^ \star & 
\varepsilon_{\mu \mu} &   \varepsilon_{\mu \tau} \\ 
{\varepsilon_{e \tau}}^\star & {\varepsilon_{\mu \tau}}^\star 
& \varepsilon_{\tau \tau}\\
\end{array} 
\right) \Bigg],     
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
where $U$ is the standard mixing matrix, $A = 2\sqrt{2}G_{F}N_{e}E/\ldm$ is the standard earth matter effect, and $\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}$ ($\alpha,\beta = e,\mu,\tau$) are the NSI parameters. 
Note that the off-diagonal NSI parameters are complex and have a CP phase $\varphi$ associated to them ($\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta} = |\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}|e^{i\varphi_{\alpha\beta}}$ with $\alpha \neq \beta$).
The most relevant NSI parameter for the $\mue$ ($\muebar$) transition is $\varepsilon_{e\mu}$ and $\varepsilon_{e\tau}$~\cite{Masud:2015xva}. 
We consider the effect of a single NSI parameter $\varepsilon_{e\mu} = 0.05$ and keep its phase $\varphi_{e\mu}$ zero. 
We have checked that considering $\varepsilon_{e\tau}$ gives qualitatively similar results. 


From Fig.\ \ref{fig:heatplot_acp}, we observe that the 
asymmetry $\acp$ is maximum around $\da \simeq \pm \pi/2$ and around the oscillation maxima, - with its magnitude increasing at higher oscillation maxima (as indicated by the increasing saturation of orange/blue patches). 
$\acp$ is negative (positive) roughly for $\da < 0$ ($\da>0$), as also evident from Eq.\ \ref{eq:diff}. 
Additionally, matter effects introduce slight distortions in the oscillation patterns, causing the heatmap to deviate from antisymmetry around $\delta_{13} \simeq 0$. 
With the inclusion of a sterile neutrino, the heatmap retains qualitatively similar features; however, the presence of an additional mass-squared difference, $\Delta m^2_{41} \sim \mathcal{O}(1)\,\text{eV}^2$, introduces small, rapid oscillations across the entire plot (second panel of Fig.\ \ref{fig:heatplot_acp}). 
Finally, the NSI parameter $\varepsilon_{e\mu}$ amplifies the matter effects and tends to enhance the value of $\acp$ at higher energies, as evidenced by the appearance of pronounced orange/blue patches even beyond 3\,GeV. 

Fig. \ref{fig:heatplot_ncp} demonstrates that as compared to DUNE, THEIA offers  cleaner sensitivity to CP effects, especially at the seond oscillation maximum. 
This is evident from the appearance of two clear peaks in the bottom row at $\om{1}$ and $\om{2}$ separated by a prominent dip (blue/white patch) irrespective of the value of $\da$. 
As observed for probability, the heatplot for $\ncp$ also shows small rapid oscillatory patterns in presence of a sterile neutrino. 
NSI enhances the matter effects and increases the asymmetry, especially at higher energies,- as observed by the extension  of the red/yellow patches upto $\gtrsim 4$ GeV. 
Overall, we note that the heatplots for THEIA shows smoother transitions and also probe more substructures,- which is a further manifestation of its better energy resolution. 


