    \caption{\footnotesize{Similar to Fig.\ \ref{fig:contour1_nh} but for inverted mass hierarchy.     
    }}
    \label{fig:contour1_ih}
\end{figure}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The final estimate of the $\chisq$ as a function of the set of desired parameters ($\bar{p}^{\text{fit}}$) for a given set of fixed parameters $p^{\text{data}}$ is obtained 
after minimizing the entire quantity within the square bracket in Eq.\ \ref{eq:chisq} over the relevant set of rest of the \textit{fit} oscillation parameters $p^{\text{fit}}-\bar{p}^{\text{fit}}$, as well as over the systematics $\eta$. 
This minimization is also referred to as marginalization over the set $\{p^{\text{fit}}-\bar{p}^{\text{fit}}; \eta\}$.  
Technically, this  procedure is the frequentist method of hypotheses testing~\cite{Fogli:2002pt, Qian:2012zn}.

In Figs.\ \ref{fig:contour1_nh} and \ref{fig:contour1_ih} we 
show the $\chisq$ contours at $1\sigma$ and $3\sigma$ confidence levels (C.L.) for 2 degrees of freedom  (D.O.F.) by considering NH and IH as true respectively.
We compare DUNE (blue contours) and one THEIA configuration with an energy resolution of $7\%/\sqrt{E}$ (red). 
In order to analyse the role of the second oscillation maximum $\om{2}$, 
we perform this $\chisq$ analysis using the following two energy ranges:
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{itemize}
\item $E \in [1.5, 18]$ GeV, where only the first oscillation maximum $\om{1}$ is considered (top rows of Figs.\ \ref{fig:contour1_nh} and \ref{fig:contour1_ih}).
\item $E \in [0.5, 18]$ GeV, where both the first and second oscillation maxima $\om{1}$ and $\om{2}$ is considered (bottom rows of Figs.\ \ref{fig:contour1_nh} and \ref{fig:contour1_ih}).
\end{itemize}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
The three columns show the $\chisq$ contours in the parameter spaces of $\tc-\da, \tc-|\ldm|$ and $\da-|\ldm|$ respectively. 
We have marginalised the set of oscillation parameters $\{\tb, \tc, \da, \ldm\}$, excluding those that are shown along the axes. 
For instance, in the second column of Figs.\ \ref{fig:contour1_nh} and \ref{fig:contour1_ih}, we have marginalised $\tb$ and $\da$.  

The regions within the contours are degenerate and regions outside the contours are excluded at $1\sigma$ or $3\sigma$ C.L., as the case may be. 
Clearly, THEIA is capable of excluding a larger parameter space than DUNE, - a feature more prominent at $3\sigma$ C.L. 
In particular, for the parameter $\tc$, one DUNE FD module can give rise to a $3\sigma$ degenerate contour around the wrong octant (lower octant). 
Even if the analysis is restricted to using only the first oscillation maximum $\om{1}$ (top row of Fig.\ \ref{fig:contour1_nh}), THEIA can still offer significant improvement in reducing such octant degeneracy. 
This is evident, for \eg, from the substantial shrinking of degenerate contours in the wrong octant of $\tc$ for THEIA. 
However, for an extended energy range where both the first and second oscillation maxima $\om{1}$ and $\om{2}$ are considered, 
THEIA can completely remove this degeneracy at $3\sigma$ (bottom row). 
This is due to the better capability of THEIA to identify $\om{2}$, as discussed in Secs.\ \ref{sec:prob_event} and \ref{sec:asym}.
The exclusion regions for IH scenario (Fig.\ \ref{fig:contour1_ih}) also demonstrate the superior sensitivity  of THEIA to the parameter space. 

Fig.\ \ref{fig:contour2} compares the sensitivity contours at $3\sigma$ for DUNE and three other THEIA configurations with the three different choices of energy resolutions as shown. 
Note that in Fig.\ \ref{fig:contour2}, DUNE and THEIA($7\%$) contours in the top (bottom) row are identical to those in the bottom row of 
Fig.\ \ref{fig:contour1_nh} (Fig.\ \ref{fig:contour1_ih}). 
Fig.\ \ref{fig:contour2} demonstrates that the THEIA contours get only slightly bigger with decrease in energy resolution upto $10\%/\sqrt{E}$. 
This implies that even with a relatively moderate energy resolution of $10\%/\sqrt{E}$, THEIA can offer significant improvement over DUNE in probing the oscillation parameter space. 
