\section{Parameter reconstruction and precision}
\label{sec:precision}
%==================================
In Fig.\ \ref{fig:reconstruct}, we demonstrate the capability 
of THEIA (with $7\%/\sqrt{E}$ energy resolution) to reconstruct the true values of the oscillation parameters $\tc, \da$ and $\ldm$ for NH and IH at $1\sigma$ and $3\sigma$ C.L. (for 1 D.O.F.), and compare it with that of DUNE. 
The horizontal axes show the true values (in the global $3\sigma$ best fit range, as tabulated in Table\ \ref{tab:parameters}), while the vertical axes show the values reconstructed by THEIA and DUNE. 
In general, we observe the improvement in reconstruction by using THEIA over DUNE, but the degree of precision depends on the true value of the oscillation parameters (especially for $\tc$ and $\da$). 
For instance, it is difficult to precisely reconstruct $\da$ if CP violation is near maximal ($\simeq \pm \pi/2$), and THEIA can visibly improve the precision around that region. 
On the other hand, it is relatively easier to reconstruct a CP conserving $\da$, and THEIA can improve the precision only slightly there. 
The observations remain very similar for NH and IH.

We further analyze the precision to reconstruct the parameters, by analyzing the error or uncertainty. 
The error in reconstruction can be defined as the one half of the reconstructed $1\sigma$ interval (1 d.o.f.) for the corresponding parameter~\cite{Coloma:2012wq} (\ie, half of the vertical width of the dark red/dark blue contours in Fig.\ \ref{fig:reconstruct} for each true value of the parameter in the standard 3 neutrino scenario). 
In Fig.\ \ref{fig:precision} we plot the errors $\Delta \tc, \Delta \da, \Delta |\ldm|$ (for the oscillation parameters $\tc, \da, |\ldm|$ respectively) 
for reconstruction by THEIA (with $7\%/\sqrt{E}$ energy resolution) and DUNE. 
As before, in addition to the standard 3-neutrino case, we consider the effect of the presence of a eV-scale sterile neutrino and that of NC NSI. 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{figure}[thb!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.8]{chisq_th23_d13_dm31_reconstruct.pdf}
    \caption{\footnotesize{This shows the capability of one THEIA FD module (red) with $7\%$ 
    energy resolution, and one DUNE FD module (blue) to reconstruct the oscillation parameters $\tc, \da$ and $|\ldm|$. The dark (light) contours correspond to $1\sigma$ ($3\sigma$) C.L. contours at 1 d.o.f.
    }}
    \label{fig:reconstruct}
\end{figure}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

For $\tc$ we observe that the error $\Delta \tc$ in the standard case (solid red and solid blue curves in the first column of Fig.\ \ref{fig:precision}) increases (\ie, precision worsens) as the maximal mixing ($\tc \to \pi/4$) is approached. 
In order to qualitatively understand the feature of $\Delta \tc$, we first note that the typical shape of the corresponding $\chisq$ is governed by the $\mumu$ disappearance channel~\cite{Coloma:2014kca}. 
Using the expression of $\pmumu$ from Ref.\ \cite{Coloma:2014kca} after neglecting the higher order terms, 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:prob_mm}
    P_{\mu\mu} \simeq 1-\sin^{2}2\tc\sin^{2}\Delta_{31} + \mathcal{O}(\alpha),
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
where $\Delta_{31}$ and $\alpha$ are the same as in Eq.\ \ref{eq:prob}.
We emphasize the crucial observation that $\pmumu$ is mostly blind to the octant ($\sin2\tc = \sin2(\frac{\pi}{2}-\tc)$) and consequently the corresponding $\chisq$ produces two solutions,- in two octants.
Now, using similar techniques as in Ref.\ \cite{Coloma:2012wq}, we note that the corresponding $\chisq$ follows the event difference $\Delta N_{\mu\mu}$ in the disappearance channel as $\tc$ is varied:
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}
    \chisq \propto \Delta N_{\mu\mu}
    \propto \Delta \pmumu
    \propto \frac{\partial \pmumu}{\partial \tc}.\Delta \tc
    \propto (\sin4\tc\sin^{2}\Delta_{31}).\Delta \tc.
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Thus, for a fixed energy and for a fixed $\chisq$ ($1\sigma$ interval, as required for the calculation of precision), 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:precision_th23}
   \Delta \tc \propto \frac{1}{\sin4\tc}, 
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
where we show only the relevant parameters.
Thus, in our range of variation of $\tc$, it is easily seen that $\Delta \tc$ increases as $\tc \to \pi/4$. 
For values of $\tc$ very close to $\pi/4$ the two solutions obtained by the $\chisq$ analysis sometimes start to overlap, reducing the degenerate regions and slightly improving the precision~\cite{Coloma:2014kca}. 
This phenomenon is manifested by the {\it{valley}}-like region very near to $\tc \simeq \pi/4$. 
In presence of the sterile neutrino, the error $\Delta \tc$ for DUNE deteriorates more significantly (for \eg, around $\tc \simeq \pi/4$, $\Delta \tc$ increases from $1.5^{\circ}$ to $2^{\circ}$) compared to THEIA ($\Delta \tc$ increases from $1.3^{\circ}$ to $1.4^{\circ}$) (dashed blue and dashed magenta curves in the first column  of Fig.\ \ref{fig:precision}). 
NSI deteriorates the precision of $\tc$, by impacting more near the maximal mixing. 
We also note that for IH, NSI worsens the precision in a skewed manner, - much more in the lower octant (dotted orange and dotted cyan curves in the left bottom panel of Fig.\ \ref{fig:precision}). 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{figure}[tb!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.6]{chisq_precision.pdf}
    \caption{\footnotesize{This shows the precisions reachable by one DUNE FD module and one THEIA module (with $7\%$ energy resolution) in reconstructing the three oscillation parameters $\tc, \da$ and $|\ldm|$ in the three columns. Each panel shows the case of the 
    standard 3 neutrino scenario (red and blue solid curves for THEIA and DUNE respectively), and two different new physics cases, namely that of the presence of a light sterile neutrino (magenta and indigo dashed curves) and the presence of one NC NSI parameter $\eem = 0.01$ (orange and cyan dotted curves). The top (bottom) row indicates the case of normal (inverted) hierarchy. 
    }}
    \label{fig:precision}
\end{figure}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

For the error $\Delta \da$, the contribution to the corresponding $\chisq$ comes dominantly from the $\mue$ appearance channel. 
Following the prescription above we find,
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:precision_d13}
    \Delta \da \propto \bigg(\frac{\partial \pmue}{\partial \da}\bigg)^{-1} \propto \frac{1}{\sin(\Delta_{31}+\da)} \propto \frac{1}{\cos \da}.
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Note that in the last step of Eq.\ \ref{eq:precision_d13}, we have assumed an oscillation maximum (where $\sin \Delta_{31} \simeq 1$ and $\cos\Delta_{31} \simeq 0$) since the energy bins around the oscillation maxima typically provides a large statistics to the $\mue$ appearance channel. 
From Eq.\ \ref{eq:precision_d13} it is clear that in the interval $\da \in [-\pi, \pi]$, $\Delta \da$ is minimum around the CP conserving values $\da \simeq 0, \pm \pi$ and maximum around the maximally CP violating values ($\da \simeq \pm \pi/2$). 
This implies the relative difficulty in measuring the CP violating values of $\da$ as compared to CP conservation. 
For the standard case, $\Delta \da$ reaches $15^{\circ}$ for DUNE and $13^{\circ}$ approximately at CP conserving $\da$ for both mass hierarchies. 
On the other hand, for maximal CP violation at $\da \simeq -\pi/2$, $\Delta \da$ is as high as $27^{\circ}$ for DUNE and $21^{\circ}$ for THEIA for normal hierarchy (solid curves in the top, middle panel of Fig.\ \ref{fig:precision}). 
This implies, that if nature turns out to be maximally CP violating with $\da \simeq -\pi/2$, THEIA can reconstruct that value within an interval of $[-111^{\circ}, -69^{\circ}]$, while DUNE can do that in $[-117^{\circ}, -63^{\circ}]$ at $1\sigma$ C.L. 
We also note that the error $\Delta \da$ cannot be improved better than roughly $12^{\circ}$, - emphasizing the overall difficulty in measuring the CP phase in neutrino oscillation experiments. 
Presence of sterile neutrino or NC NSI worsens the precision to some extent, and introduces minor shift of the peaks and troughs. 
