For the atmospheric mass-squared difference $\ldm$, the 
corresponding error can be seen as,
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:precision_dm31}
\Delta |\ldm| 
\propto \bigg(\frac{\partial \pmue}{\partial |\ldm|} \bigg)^{-1}
\propto \frac{E}{\sin2\Delta_{31}},
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
which is mostly uniform as long as $\ldm$ is restricted to a moderate interval such as in our case. 
This is seen in the third column of Fig.\ \ref{fig:precision}. 
Overall, we note that $\Delta |\ldm|$ remains very precise, even in presence of new physics and is always less than $2 \times 10^{-5} \text{ eV}^{2}$ approximately. 
In other words, to measure a typical value of $\ldm \simeq 2.45 \times 10^{-3} \text{ eV}^{2}$, even in the worst case scenario, DUNE can zero in on a quite precise interval of $[2.43, 2.47] \times 10^{-3} \text{ eV}^{2}$ at $1\sigma$ C.L.

Note that, in our analytical discussion of the precision, we have neglected correlations among oscillation parameters and employed vacuum probability expressions for simplicity. Additionally, spectral information and systematic uncertainties have been omitted in favor of a qualitative understanding of the numerically generated figures. For a more comprehensive treatment that includes some of these effects, we refer the reader to Refs.~\cite{Coloma:2012wq, Coloma:2014kca}.
%============================
\section{Summary and discussions}
\label{sec:summary}
%===============================
While three of the four FD modules of DUNE will most likely be liquid argon time projection 
chambers (LArTPC), the type of the fourth detector module is still under discussion in the community. 
In this work we have explored the possibility of using an alternative FD module for DUNE,- namely water based liquid scintillator (WbLS), - known as THEIA in the community. 
The WbLS detection technology, which is a relatively new concept, employs an optimized 
cocktail of pure water and organic scintillator. 
Consequently, it can detect both scintillation and Cherenkov radiation. 
Detection of scintillation provides WbLS a lower energy threshold and a superior energy resolution (potentially as small as $3\%/\sqrt{E}$), 
- crucial for precision measurements of the neutrino oscillation parameters. 
On the other hand, Cherenkov radiation enables angular resolution, - which helps in identifying 
neutrinos coming from astrophysical sources. 

In Sec.\ \ref{sec:prob_event} we begin by analytically deriving the locations of the peaks and dips in the $\mue$ oscillation. 
The first  and second oscillation maxima ($\om{1}$ and $\om{2}$ respectively) in vacuum occur at approximately $E_{1}^\text{OM} \simeq 2.5$ GeV and $E_{2}^\text{OM} = E_{1}^\text{OM}/3 \simeq 0.8$ GeV respectively.  
We compute the CP asymmetry $\acp = (\pmue-\pmuebar)$ and show that this quantity is three times larger at
 $\om{2}$ than at $\om{1}$. 
 We present the event spectra considering one WbLS FD module (using three different energy resolutions as $3\%/\sqrt{E}, 7\%/\sqrt{E}, 10\%/\sqrt{E}$, - collectively referred to as THEIA hereafter) and compare them with a single LArTPC FD module (hereafter, referred to as DUNE). 
 The event spectra illustrate that THEIA, due to its enhanced energy resolution, can track the first and second oscillation maxima more clearly, unlike DUNE, - which cannot distinguish the two peaks as effectively. 
 Notably even with a relatively moderate energy resolution of $7\%/\sqrt{E}-10\%/\sqrt{E}$, THEIA  significantly outperforms DUNE. 
 In Sec.\ \ref{sec:asym}, we then proceed to analyze the heatplots of CP asymmetry both in the probability level $\acp$ and in the event level ($\ncp = \nmue-\nmuebar$) in the parameter space of energy and the standard CP phase $\da$. 
 We also consider the presence of two possible well-motivated new physics cases,- namely that of an eV-scale sterile neutrino and NC NSI and show how the heatplots get modified. 

 In our statistical analyses in Sec.\ \ref{sec:contour}, we illustrate that THEIA with a benchmark energy resolution of $7\%/\sqrt{E}$ can exclude the parameter space associated with the less-well known oscillation parameters $\tc, \da, \ldm$ significantly better than DUNE. 
 This is especially noticeable in reducing the wrong octant degeneracy for $\tc$.
 When the energy window for analysis is extended to include the second oscillation maximum, THEIA performs even better to fully lift the octant degeneracy at $3\sigma$. 
 Our analysis assuming both the normal and inverted hierarchies show qualitatively similar features. 
 Moreover, we find that THEIA?s statistical performance remains robust even at energy resolutions up to  $10\%/\sqrt{E}$.

 Sec.\ \ref{sec:precision} presents a comparative statistical  performance of THEIA and DUNE in reconstructing the oscillation parameters $\tc, \da, \ldm$ at $1\sigma$ and $3\sigma$ C.L. for both NH and IH cases. 
 We quantify the precision by defining the error in reconstruction as the one half of the $1\sigma$ reconstructed interval, and analyze its dependence on the true values of the oscillation parameters. 
 The error $\Delta \tc$ sharply increases as $\tc \to \pi/4$, indicating the general difficulty in measuring  near maximal mixing. 
 We also derive simple analytical expressions from probability to understand the behavior of the error $\Delta \tc$. 
 We find that in presence of a light sterile neutrino, the error $\Delta \tc$ deteriorates to $2^{\circ}$ for DUNE (from $1.5^{\circ}$ in the standard three-neutrino case) and $1.4^{\circ}$ for THEIA (from $1.3^{\circ}$) around $\tc \simeq \pi/4$, - 
 implying the significantly better precision achievable by THEIA even when new physics is present. 
 The CP phase $\da$ can be reconstructed with moderate precision with an error $\Delta \da$ ranging from $13^{\circ}-21^{\circ}$ ($15^{\circ}-27^{\circ}$) for THEIA (DUNE) approximately. 
 The error $\Delta \da$ is sinusoidal, with a minima around CP conserving values of $\da$, and peaking towards maximal CP violation, - a behaviour which can be well understood by our analytical expression ($\Delta \da \propto 1/\cos\da$). 
 In addition to slightly shifting the location of peaks/dips of $\Delta \da$, the presence of sterile neutrino in general increases the error $\Delta \da$ (close to $35^{\circ}$ for DUNE and $25^{\circ}$ for THEIA near maximal CP violation). 
 The error $\Delta |\ldm|$ on the other hand remains highly constrained even in presence of new physics ($\lesssim 2\times 10^{-5} \text{ eV}^{2}$) and is mostly uniform. 

 In conclusion, we find that a WbLS-based detector like THEIA,- even with a relatively moderate energy resolution of $7 - 10\%/\sqrt{E}$,- offers clear advantages over DUNE's LArTPC-based FD module in measuring standard oscillation parameters both in standard 3-neutrino framework and in presence of new physics such as sterile neutrino and NC NSI. 
 %This improvement in performance is manifested in excluding the standard oscillation parameter space associated to the yet unknown parameters $\ta, \da, \ldm$, as well as in  reconstructing the allowed true values of these parameters. 
 %In presence of possible new physics, such improvement is even more visible. 
 Our findings aim to inform the decision-making process regarding the fourth DUNE FD module by providing a quantitative physics-driven comparison of LArTPC and WbLS technologies, under both standard and BSM frameworks.
 If the fourth DUNE FD module is indeed installed with a WbLS detector under the THEIA project, DUNE will be able to leverage the complementary strengths of both WbLS and LArTPCs (from the three other FD modules), offering an unprecedented hybrid sensitivity to the oscillation parameters.
