\subsection{An anti-dispersion mass lumping technique}
One commonly faced difficulty when solving Vlasov--Maxwell equations using numerical schemes is that Vlasov--Maxwell equations can be high-dimensional, which significantly increases the computation burden. This is also one important factor to be considered in our FE framework. Let $\polM:=\polM^x\otimes\polM^v$, and our primary computational cost lies in solving the following system
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ls}
  \dot{\polf}=\polM\backslash(\polA^{\pmb{x}}\otimes\polC^{\pmb{v}}+\polC^\bE\otimes \polA^{\pmb{v}}+\polC^\bB\otimes \polA^{\pmb{\times}}+\mathsf{A}_{h,{\pmb{x}}}(\polD^{\pmb{x}}\otimes \polM^v)+\mathsf{A}_{h,{\pmb{v}}}(\polM^x\otimes \polD^{\pmb{v}}))\polf.
\end{equation}
The mass matrix $\polM$ is sparse, while inverting it is still computationally expensive when a large number of degrees of freedom are applied. We utilize the method introduced in \cite{MR3002788} to accelerate our scheme. The method works as follows: first we define the matrix $\polA_l$ which is given by
\begin{equation}
    \polA_l= (\overline{\polM}-\polM)\overline{\polM}^{-1},\notag
\end{equation}
where $\overline{\polM}$ is the lumped mass matrix: $\overline{\polM} = {\rm diag}(m_1,\cdots,m_N)$. Then the inversed mass matrix can be represented as follows
\begin{equation}
  \polM^{-1}=\overline{\polM}^{-1}(\polI+\polA_l+\polA_l^2+\cdots)\notag
\end{equation}

